Every reducer test should assert two things beyond the expected state change: **determinism** (same inputs, same output across runs) and **snapshot isolation** (the document you passed in is unchanged afterwards).

```ts
test("completing a todo marks it done", () => {
  let doc = utils.createDocument();
  doc = reducer(doc, addTodo({ id: "t-1", title: "Test" }));
  const afterComplete = reducer(doc, completeTodo({ id: "t-1" }));

  expect(afterComplete.state.global.items[0].done).toBe(true);
  expect(doc.state.global.items[0].done).toBe(false); // original snapshot untouched
});
```

The second assertion is the one people skip, and it is the one that catches real bugs. It works because the reducer core runs your operation inside Mutative's `create()`, which hands you a draft and returns a fresh document — the input object is never written through.

So the assertion is not testing Mutative. It is testing **your** reducer, by detecting the one class of impurity Mutative cannot protect you from: reaching outside the draft. Capturing a reference to a nested object across calls, mutating an array that came from module scope or from `action.input`, or holding state in a closure all produce a reducer that writes to memory the draft does not cover. Those writes leak into the input snapshot, and this assertion is what surfaces them.

Why it matters more than a typical purity test: an impure reducer does not fail locally. It fails when a peer replays the log and lands on different state, or when the recomputed hash stops matching. Sync will eventually betray you — the snapshot assertion is how you find out in CI instead.

Note this is distinct from asserting on a *failed* operation: when a reducer throws, the operation is still appended with `.error` set and state is left unchanged.

_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/02-Learn/08-deep-dives/01-reducers-in-depth.mdx, confirmed against packages/shared/document-model/reducer.ts (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._
